WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.210
About 18 months ago, I made
a trip up to Eugene, Oregon,

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.873
back when we would do such things.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.930
I went to go see a guy
named Mark Frohnmayer.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.950
He's the founder and CEO

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.870
of a company called Arcimoto,

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.380
which makes this very cool

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.470
and kind of different electric vehicle.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.197
They call it a Fun Utility Vehicle, a FUV.

00:00:28.707 --> 00:00:31.010
It's super fun to drive,

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.993
and I took it all around Eugene.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.640
The company, after this
really long struggle,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.840
had started to hit its stride.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.360
Its manufacturing plant
was up and running.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.510
They knocked out the first 100 vehicles

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.020
and started delivering
them all up and down

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.950
the West Coast of the United States.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.240
And then, of course, the
coronavirus came along,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.030
and slowed everything down for them.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:57.670
But if there's a bright
spot in any of this,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.850
at least for Arcimoto,
it's that their vehicles

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.700
have turned out to be really
useful during the pandemic

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.640
and maybe to have opened
up some new opportunities

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.540
for the company.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.620
And so with that, let
us head up to Eugene,

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.853
and Mark's house, and
check in on Arcimoto.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.910
Ah-ha, I get to take a tour of your house.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:20.743
Yup, right?

00:01:20.743 --> 00:01:22.640
That's one of the rare
perks of doing this.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.378
We cleaned up the kitchen just for you.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.300
This is the one you've
had since the start?

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.030
This is VIN number zero.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.860
I don't know if you can read that.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.610
I don't know if this is too awkward,

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.000
but can you sort of
flip your screen around,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.790
so that somebody who's never seen

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.060
one of these before can
take a peak at the console?

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.990
So you got the, it's a fairly bare bones

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.580
screen shows your state of charge.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.060
The phone, you put your,
so if you want navigation,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.600
you just have it on your phone right here

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.090
in the middle of the handlebars,

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.060
and then all your controls
are on your hands.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.610
So you've got, it really,

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.140
the vehicle is, I think, that combination

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.460
of having something that's
really, really fun to drive,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.300
and is practical for daily trips,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.770
and at the same time makes

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.300
a meaningful statement
about sustainability.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.180
I dig the red, man.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.130
It's a sweet ride.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.930
But I've got to assume you
guys have been struggling

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.240
a little bit to get some of the new ones

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.930
that you've made out to customers.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.600
Well, we've been doing some kind of crazy

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.860
California road trips.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.430
We, the one way we figured
out we could do deliveries,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.957
just for some very key ones,
was we got a motor home.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.120
We're loading up the very first delivery

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.780
outside of Arcimoto for
commercial purposes.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.220
We've enclosed our COVID
bubble on the road.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.190
One nice thing about driving in California

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:48.460
in the middle of the pandemic is

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.370
it's the very first time I've ever seen

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.280
Google Maps be all green.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.370
It is one of the only nice
things to come out of this.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.143
Get all those gas cars off the road!

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.890
Hey, Mark, what's up, buddy?

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:04.900
Just wanted to let you
know how much I am enjoying

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.530
my amazing FUV Arcimoto.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.990
It is an absolute blast.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:13.070
How did this stuff with
Mark Wahlberg even come up?

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.010
He was actually, he's a business partner

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.380
of one of our key investors.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.290
He, you know, he used to ride motorcycles,

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.390
but then has kids,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.993
and decided that was not a wise idea.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.490
So the last time I saw you in person,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.520
it was probably like 18 months ago,

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.460
and you guys have been
on this long journey.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.960
You'd finally got the
factory up and running

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.703
and hit your stride.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.200
You know, what's been the story

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.200
of the last 18 months for Arcimoto?

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.100
We've now been in production

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.350
for a little bit less than a year,

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:54.350
and we've put more than 100
production FUVs on the road.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:59.920
We have launched our pilots
of the Rapid Responder,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.520
which is our vehicle
for emergency services,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.420
law enforcement, campus
security, and the Deliverator,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.600
which is our vehicle
for last mile delivery.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.960
We put those pilots on the road in March,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.220
and then of course,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.650
we're having this
discussion from our houses,

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:20.973
because we're in the middle of a pandemic.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.810
And that has been, you know,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.270
if you think of the production hell

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.120
of beginning the scaling process,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.320
and then add in a global pandemic

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.670
that has disrupted supply chains,

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.250
made our own manufacturing processes,

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.640
we've really had to rethink

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.400
how we space people out
within the facility.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.110
All of that's been going
on at the same time

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.880
that I think our, the
vehicles that we're building

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.354
have become, if anything, more relevant

00:04:52.354 --> 00:04:55.090
to the world that we're facing.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.030
What's been your personal
experience through the pandemic?

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.180
Because I would think it's got
to be incredibly frustrating.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.500
You get the company to this good point,

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.590
you get the manufacturing line humming,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.850
the customers are starting
to get their deliveries,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:12.102
and then this crisis sets in.

00:05:12.102 --> 00:05:14.100
How have you coped with that?

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.670
I think the frustrating part for me

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.780
is just that we have a lot of customers

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.750
who've been waiting for a very long time

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.960
to get their Arcimotos,
and we've had to, you know,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.780
hold off on delivering them,
and finishing them, and so on.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.360
If anything, if this venture
has taught me anything,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.080
it is some measure of patience.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.810
You know, I've been at
it for almost 13 years.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.890
And so, while that has been a frustration,

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:43.400
I think the sort of silver lining of it,

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.940
at least for the company,

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.770
is to have a chance to really focus

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.540
on what it takes to get to scale.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.210
And that's important for us, really,

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.170
well, for every reason.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.830
But primarily, if we
actually want to succeed

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:02.560
in our mission, which is
to make a meaningful impact

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.700
on climate change and
transportation emissions,

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.410
we've got to make it
a lot of these things.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.320
We're shooting for a production
rate of 50,000 vehicles

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.190
a year in 24 months.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.573
That's what takes us from the
realm of sort of a boutique,

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.560
niche vehicle provider to really providing

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.030
a mass market solution.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.800
And so, taking the time to figure that out

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.960
while the world is sort of shut down

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.283
is a good use of time.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:31.540
One of the crazier things
or the pandemic has been

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:34.080
that the stock market's been going up.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.080
Tesla, in particular, has
basically been going to the moon,

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:43.030
and its stock is sort of
borderline ridiculous right now.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:44.400
The bull market and Tesla,

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.220
it's real, and it's spectacular.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.100
It's gone from $361 to almost 1,800.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.860
Tesla could really be stock of the month

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.120
and stock of the year.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.620
You have to kind of decide for yourself

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.670
whether this is rational or not,

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.910
but without doubt, the
rise of Tesla has had

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.260
this knock-on effect of making

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:09.080
all the electric vehicle
makers' stocks shoot up as well.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.930
And I was just kind of curious
what you make of all this.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.310
Almost every, so if you
look at the basket of stocks

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.650
that we're in, everyone
from NIO to Nikola to SOLO

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.590
to Workhorse, they've all just, you know,

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.273
gone right up the hockey stick.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.740
And I think that is
indicative of a few things.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.617
One is that this is the first time

00:07:34.617 --> 00:07:37.580
for some people in their lifetimes

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.550
where they've seen what
clean skies look like.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:43.230
And the electric vehicle is

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:47.450
what gets us there from a
transportation standpoint.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:51.560
And I think also the ideas around

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.100
how we're gonna transport
ourselves have changed.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.350
You know, when's the next time you plan

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.800
to jump on a plane?

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.280
When's the next time you plan,

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.130
people are are working from home.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:04.870
They're, so your need to
commute long distance,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:09.530
your need to fly long distance,
has gone away largely.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.320
And instead, it's all about
how can I enjoy my local area,

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.920
my local community, get
to the grocery store,

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.420
go out in the country
for a ride, or whatever.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:23.750
I think if the pandemic, if
I had one takeaway from it,

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.740
it is that it, for me,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.750
it fundamentally dispelled the notion

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.280
that people can't change.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.840
Everything changed in the
span of a very short period

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:37.430
of time once people realized
that there was a grave threat.

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:40.780
And what, that actually
really gives me hope

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.370
that when people see the enormity

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.710
of the threat posed by climate change,

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.110
that we will begin making the big steps

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:51.870
that we need to make together,

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.470
in order to address the problem.

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.310
You're a busy guy, and so, you know,

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:01.610
very busy, in addition to all
the electric vehicle work.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:04.400
I mean, you've been working on a new

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.130
sort of voting system
for a while now, right?

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.880
So I had a front row seat
to the political process.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.620
I was actually born into my
dad's very first campaign

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.210
for the state legislature,

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.899
and then he went on to
hold statewide office.

00:09:18.899 --> 00:09:21.883
Dad would be a great governor.

00:09:21.883 --> 00:09:22.950
Dave Frohnmayer,

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.770
a lot of people really like him.

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.910
And what became really
clear to me over time

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.940
is that we have a super
broken political system.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.010
What I found is that there's
a little bug in how we vote

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.110
that leads to the
polarization that we see,

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:40.530
and that bug is that in every election

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.300
we're limited to a single choice.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:47.050
So what STAR Voting aims
to do is fix that bug

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:50.440
by giving an equally-weighted
vote to all the voters.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:54.430
It shifts the choice from
one choice per office

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.490
to actually letting you have an opinion,

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:59.210
a zero to five star opinion,

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.930
on every candidate for
a particular office.

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.719
I mean, can you give me
just the bare bone basics

00:10:04.719 --> 00:10:06.707
on how this works-

00:10:06.707 --> 00:10:08.620
Yeah, absolutely.
And what STAR stands for?

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:09.960
So STAR is an acronym,

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.820
as it represents the
ubiquitous five star, you know,

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.840
zero stars, bad, five stars, good rating

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:18.700
that we're all familiar
with from everything

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.600
from apps to Amazon, but the acronym means

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.500
Score Then Automatic Runoff.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:25.340
All the scores get added up.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:29.340
The two candidates who scored
the most over all the people,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.830
those are the finalists,

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.190
and then your vote simply goes

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:36.712
to whichever one of those
two you gave a higher score.

00:10:36.712 --> 00:10:39.920
Okay, so if there's like six candidates,

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.900
I can go in, if I want, I could do it

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:45.250
just like a regular election
and just give my favorite five,

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:49.220
and leave everyone zero, or I could go in,

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.970
give my favorite five, and
then maybe I kind of liked

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:53.330
the policies of two of the other people.

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:55.750
I can actually like give
them like a four star

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.120
or a three star, or something in between-

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.510
Yeah, you could go five,
four, four, one, zero.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.730
It's totally sort of backward compatible

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:03.990
with how we vote now.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.430
If you're like, "Ah
that's too complicated,"

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:08.580
you just give you one
favorite a five, and move on.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:10.420
That's a totally valid vote.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.620
But if you have an opinion

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:15.880
about more than one candidate
running, you can express it.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:18.980
To whatever level of support
you wanna give a candidate,

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:19.813
you can do that.

00:11:19.813 --> 00:11:22.450
Where have we seen it in practice so far?

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:24.650
It was used for the very first time

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:28.950
in a statewide political election
here in Oregon this year.

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:30.920
Almost every single person who was able

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:33.830
to get into the system used it.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:35.950
Voters, when given the opportunity,

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.490
to have a more nuanced
viewpoint on the outcome,

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:41.810
actually do use that capability.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:45.060
And is it better suited for any particular

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.540
kind of election, or is this something

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.410
you think is universal and
you guys would like to see it

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.710
be used in presidential elections?

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.025
Dog catcher to president.

00:11:54.025 --> 00:11:54.900
Yeah.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.560
But we think it's gonna
bring immediate benefit

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:59.750
at whatever level of government it's used.

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:03.080
And ultimately, the goal is
to heal the political divide

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:05.700
that we face as a people.

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:09.900
Two polarized parties, each
of which are aggregations

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:13.570
of special interests trying
to get to 50% plus one,

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:15.760
because, and that's what pockets

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.270
things that should not
be political issues,

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.650
like the work to address climate change

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.630
should not be a political issue.

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:26.450
Racial justice should
not be a political issue.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:28.690
But they end up getting loaded

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.380
into one or the other party,

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:34.210
where each party sort of has to oppose

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.180
the position that the
other party is taking.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.800
We are not a polarized people.

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:40.720
We have a polarizing process

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:44.170
that is driving a wedge
through the country.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:47.020
And I think it's to our great
peril that that's the case.

